WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.370
There are over 800 episodes in the Star Trek
franchise, and while most of those are filled

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:09.639
with amazing adventures, a few stories fail
completely and problematically.

00:00:09.639 --> 00:00:12.519
Keep watching to learn the times that Star
Trek went too far.

00:00:13.199 --> 00:00:15.380
When Star Trek: Picard was first announced

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:20.320
in 2019, Patrick Stewart warned fans during
a university visit that it might not be the

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.550
continuation of Star Trek: The Next Generation
they expected.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:29.140
And shocked they were when Star Trek: Voyager
fan-favorite character Icheb, the escaped

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.030
Borg child from the Delta Quadrant who returned
home with the ship to the Federation, was

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:38.690
unceremoniously killed off in the opening
moments of "Stardust City Rag."

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:43.300
Not only did many fans see it as a slap in
the face to kill off the beloved character,

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.270
but how they did it went too far.

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:49.550
The episode opens with a villainous doctor
brutally torturing Icheb — who’s now a

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:54.859
Starfleet Lieutenant — and ripping out his
eye in a gory and graphic scene.

00:00:54.859 --> 00:00:59.920
The irony is that she is seeking his cortical
node, which Icheb gave to Seven when her life

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.069
was in danger in the Voyager episode "Imperfection."

00:01:03.069 --> 00:01:08.000
It's a nice bit of continuity, and Icheb's
death does help serve to motivate Seven's

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.010
character appropriately.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:14.530
That said, the blood-soaked torture scene,
with Icheb's visceral screams, seemed woefully

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:19.460
out of place and unnecessarily graphic, even
for the more adult-skewing Picard.

00:01:20.276 --> 00:01:22.450
Star Trek: The Motion Picture was the first

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:27.479
live-action glimpse at Kirk, Spock, McCoy,
and the Enterprise that audiences had gotten

00:01:27.479 --> 00:01:28.870
in a decade.

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:33.670
It ushered in a new era of family-friendly
movies and television shows, which would run

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.600
uninterrupted for more than twenty-five years.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:42.039
But one moment in that seminal film still
stands as darker and more horrifying than

00:01:42.039 --> 00:01:44.420
anything else seen in the franchise.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:50.039
With the Enterprise undergoing its refit,
two officers are beamed up to the ship.

00:01:50.039 --> 00:01:55.399
But the transporter malfunctions and deforms
the pair into a terrifying mass of half-transported

00:01:55.399 --> 00:01:56.780
flesh.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:01.409
The cries of agony from the victims can be
heard echoing through the transporter room.

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:06.369
Thankfully, the audience is spared a graphic
look at the results as they are beamed immediately

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:09.869
back to Earth, after which the transporter
chief tells Kirk:

00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:10.869
"Enterprise.

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:16.629
What we got back didn't live long ... fortunately."

00:02:16.629 --> 00:02:21.100
It's a shocking scene that feels out of place
in what is otherwise a family-friendly Star

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.100
Trek movie.

00:02:22.372 --> 00:02:25.440
In an episode that dealt with issues of identity,

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.180
Vulcan security chief Tuvok is caught in a
transporter accident with the ship's chef

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:31.650
Neelix.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:36.180
Unlike the horrific accident in The Motion
Picture, the pair aren't deformed but merged

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.180
into one new, sentient being: Tuvix.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:44.409
Logical like Tuvok and carefree like Neelix,
he's an entirely new person.

00:02:44.409 --> 00:02:49.240
He’s a compelling character in his own right,
who under other circumstances might have been

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:52.019
a welcome full-time addition to the show.

00:02:52.019 --> 00:02:56.990
But the Doctor discovers a way to reverse
the accident and bring back Tuvok and Neelix.

00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:00.580
The only problem is that it would mean essentially
killing Tuvix.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:05.439
The new being refuses the procedure on the
grounds that he's his own person now and entitled

00:03:05.439 --> 00:03:07.019
to exist.

00:03:07.019 --> 00:03:11.209
Even the Doctor says it's a violation of his
medical ethics to perform the procedure, leaving

00:03:11.209 --> 00:03:13.159
Captain Janeway to do it.

00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:17.629
A heartbreaking moment occurs on the bridge
when Tuvix begs someone, anyone, to stand

00:03:17.629 --> 00:03:20.019
up and stop the captain from killing him.

00:03:20.019 --> 00:03:24.270
It’s a powerful moment because none of the
crew respond to his pleas, instead choosing

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.494
to respect their captain’s decision.

00:03:27.019 --> 00:03:29.910
"Code of Honor" has been spoken about endlessly,

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:33.769
and those involved with the early first season
episode of Star Trek: The Next Generation

00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:38.600
have mentioned their regret and embarrassment
over it many times throughout the years.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:42.680
But "Code of Honor" is simply too far over
the line of decency to ignore.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:47.620
And it’s a lot more than just one scene,
moment, or character that goes too far.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:52.370
The entire episode is packed with racism and
misogyny, with the backward alien culture

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:57.120
portrayed by all Black actors with heavy accents
fighting to the death in primitive ritual

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.650
combat over a white woman.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:04.230
And there's seemingly no lesson here, no moral
dilemma, or ethical problem that justifies

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.549
what we see.

00:04:05.549 --> 00:04:10.049
There was no attempt to use racial stereotypes
to tell the audience about the awful truth

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:11.750
of intolerance, either.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:16.769
Exposing that intolerance might have mitigated
the uncomfortable nature of the portrayal.

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:21.570
What's worse, it's just a poorly written,
poorly acted, and all-around bad episode,

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:24.050
making it an easy skip on any series rewatch.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.840
On Star Trek: Enterprise, Captain Archer was

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:31.080
a Kirk-like leader who would command an away
mission and was unafraid to buck protocol.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.540
But, like Kirk, he was also a morally upstanding
captain.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:39.300
He always tried to do the right thing, help
those in need, and see the best in every situation,

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.620
no matter how dire it may have looked.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:46.419
That's why his dramatic shift during the third
season's "Xindi War" story arc was so striking

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:48.229
and crossed the line for many fans.

00:04:48.229 --> 00:04:53.450
When a mysterious alien race commits an unprovoked
attack on Earth, killing millions, the Enterprise

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:56.360
is dispatched to find the Xindi.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:00.520
During the story, Archer goes from a man who
always does the right thing to a violent captain

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:04.720
willing to break every Starfleet rule in service
of revenge.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:09.169
They try to justify his actions by saying
that the Xindi are intent on wiping out all

00:05:09.169 --> 00:05:14.030
of humanity, but on an episode-by-episode
basis it often doesn't add up.

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:19.110
Produced in a post-9/11 world, Archer's turn
mirrored many real-life shifts in public thinking

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:20.490
at the time.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:24.949
But for Star Trek, known for its unyielding
optimism, it went too far.

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:28.480
There were other options than corrupting one
of Starfleet's greatest captains.

00:05:29.024 --> 00:05:31.090
The final episode of the original series,

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:35.640
"Turnabout Intruder" sank as low it could
go into the waters of misogyny.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:39.860
Captain Kirk encounters his ex-lover, Janice
Lester, and there are problems right away

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:44.700
when she says:
"Your world of starship captains doesn't admit

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:45.700
women."

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:49.600
Fans have sometimes interpreted this as Starfleet
not allowing women to be captains, and they

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:50.600
may be right.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:54.889
She implies that it's why she has never been
able to achieve a command position.

00:05:54.889 --> 00:05:59.070
She uses a strange device to swap bodies with
Kirk, forcing her former lover to experience

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:02.949
the abuse and marginalization that she has
endured.

00:06:02.949 --> 00:06:06.770
Throughout the story, Lester-as-Kirk, who’s
intent on killing the crew, attempts to rule

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:09.539
the Enterprise with a conniving, petty fist.

00:06:09.539 --> 00:06:14.759
She turns the tough, skilled, respected Captain
Kirk into a petulant and whiny brat.

00:06:14.759 --> 00:06:19.030
This sends the message that women are incompetent
and incapable of leadership, while men alone

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:21.800
are strong, proficient, and worthy of command.

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:27.430
No one has ever figured out if this was Roddenberry's
own bias or simply a bad script gone wrong,

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:28.849
but it goes way, way too far.

00:06:29.529 --> 00:06:31.710
If you thought "Turnabout Intruder" was bad,

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:36.160
the Star Trek: Deep Space Nine episode "Profit
and Lace" is even worse.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:40.759
Rather than use the sexist state of Ferengi
culture to provide commentary on sexism in

00:06:40.759 --> 00:06:44.770
the real world, "Profit and Lace" plays on
the worst gender stereotypes.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:50.880
It makes a mockery not just of women, but
of the LGBTQ community, and trans people specifically.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.400
The episode uses a gender swap as a plot device
for Quark to get what he wants.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:59.190
And even though the writers try to have him
learn a lesson about how to treat women, it’s

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:03.440
ruined by the implication that it's only the
residual results of his transition.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:07.599
While it is noble that the show tried to say
something about women's rights, on Ferenginar

00:07:07.599 --> 00:07:10.259
at least, it's still the men who get the job
done.

00:07:10.259 --> 00:07:13.789
And though some have defended it in recent
years, it's an episode that should never have

00:07:13.789 --> 00:07:16.150
made it off the drawing board for many reasons.

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:20.259
It's a farcical episode that plays on stereotypes,
and we're comfortable saying it will never

00:07:20.259 --> 00:07:22.768
make anyone's top Star Trek episode list.

00:07:23.098 --> 00:07:25.500
We've seen Sisko taking morally gray actions

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:30.869
in the past, but in those instances, his decisions
are usually justified with grave consequences

00:07:30.869 --> 00:07:33.569
— or by millions of lives being on the line.

00:07:33.569 --> 00:07:40.509
"A guilty conscience is a small price to pay
for the safety of the Alpha Quadrant."

00:07:40.509 --> 00:07:44.229
There's little such justification for his
actions in "For the Uniform."

00:07:44.229 --> 00:07:49.560
Sisko's reasons for going too far are almost
entirely motivated by a personal vendetta.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:54.060
It all starts in the episode "For the Cause,"
when the long-time Starfleet security chief

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:56.730
is revealed as a Maquis terrorist.

00:07:56.730 --> 00:08:01.150
Captain Sisko takes this betrayal hard, not
just because Eddington defected from Starfleet,

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:04.240
but because he never suspected Eddington's
duplicity.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:08.270
When we get to "For the Uniform," Sisko is
on the hunt for Eddington, making it very

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:09.870
clear that this is personal.

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:13.620
The former security chief didn't just betray
Starfleet, he betrayed Sisko.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:17.440
The captain can't handle the fact that Eddington
beat him in their first encounter.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:22.520
Now, with Eddington threatening to poison
an entire Cardassian planet, Sisko goes way

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:23.919
too far to catch his prey.

00:08:23.919 --> 00:08:28.860
He destroys the ecosystem of an entire Maquis
world to get Eddington to surrender, nearly

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:31.580
wiping out an entire population of the planet.

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:35.470
Though the Maquis would be able to escape
and find a new home, his actions betrayed

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:37.320
every Starfleet ideal.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:40.500
It could have resulted in a massive death
toll if it went wrong.

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:43.770
And in reality, it should have seen him at
least court-martialed for his actions.

00:08:44.353 --> 00:08:46.640
Even Star Trek: The Next Generation wasn't

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:47.980
immune to misogyny.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:51.150
Too often, the women of the Enterprise were
given weak episodes.

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:56.110
Nowhere was this more evident than with Counselor
Troi, who was often either shown as bratty,

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:59.890
obsessed with food, or being distracted by
a romantic conquest.

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:03.980
She was the victim of assault or bodily violation
on more than one occasion, but it reached

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:06.630
its worst moment in Star Trek: Nemesis.

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:10.570
In the film, Troi serves little purpose other
than to become the target of the villain's

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:12.050
sexual aggression.

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:16.630
Shinzon uses the help of a powerful Reman
servant to invade Troi's mind and engage in

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:18.960
telepathic sexual assault.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.350
It's an element that has no place in the film.

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:25.460
We already understand how vile Shinzon is,
and it seems solely motivated by the producer's

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:29.410
desire for a sex scene — and perhaps to
give Troi something to do.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:33.560
Making Troi a victim once again isn't the
meaty Star Trek character arc they think it

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:34.560
is.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:36.610
The Next Generation marked the long-awaited

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:41.500
return of Star Trek to living rooms, and it
was aimed at the same family audience as the

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:42.500
original.

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:46.690
The new series was once again a bright, colorful,
optimistic look at the future with a crew

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:50.670
who all got along, fighting for peace and
equality throughout the galaxy.

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:54.800
But the first season episode "Conspiracy"
gave audiences a darker story than they might

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:58.790
have expected — a story that in hindsight
would probably have been better suited to

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:00.150
Deep Space Nine.

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:03.840
In an unsettling opening, Picard is warned
of a dire threat.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:08.321
He is told that something is wrong at Starfleet,
and it goes all the way to the top.

00:10:08.321 --> 00:10:12.440
The conspiracy comes to the Enterprise when
a high-ranking Admiral tries to kill Commander

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:16.130
Riker and implant him with an alien bug that
will control his mind.

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:20.630
In the climax, the leader of the interstellar
invasion, Commander Remmick, is revealed as

00:10:20.630 --> 00:10:22.530
the mastermind.

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:26.330
Picard and his first officer confront him
at Starfleet Headquarters.

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:30.730
Fully revealed, they proceed to blow his head
apart with phaser fire and eviscerate his

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:35.520
upper torso in a graphic scene that likely
horrified family audiences watching in 1987.

00:10:36.511 --> 00:10:39.340
The Star Trek: Enterprise episode “Similitude”

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:43.980
sees Tucker terminally wounded, along with
a clone created to help restore him to life.

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:47.610
While the clone gets accustomed to living
on the ship, he bonds with the crew in some

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:50.250
of the most beautiful moments of the series.

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:54.420
It's also the episode where T'Pol learns that
Trip is in love with her, taking their ongoing

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.650
relationship story to the next level.

00:10:56.650 --> 00:11:00.930
The problem, however, is that the clone of
Tucker is created for the sole purpose of

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:03.650
using his organs to save the original.

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:08.560
This unique form of cloning results in a being
whose lifespan is just two weeks.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:13.100
It's an ethical problem discussed only briefly,
and they once again use the Xindi War to justify

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:16.210
their creation of the clone knowing it will
die in days.

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:21.010
The heartbreaking life and death of the "Sim"
clone are what make the episode great.

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:25.460
However, we can't help but feel that bringing
a life into being simply to harvest its organs

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:27.640
was a bit too dark, and too far.

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:31.360
It could have been more interesting if the
clone brought about a discussion over the

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:33.320
implications of genetic cloning.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:37.220
Instead, it seems like a cheap, manipulative
plot device to pull at the audience's heartstrings.

00:11:38.134 --> 00:11:40.480
Star Trek has rarely shied away from addressing

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:42.770
sensitive and controversial subjects.

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:48.470
The Voyager episode "Retrospect" is no exception,
which sees Seven assaulted in an obvious rape

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:49.490
allegory.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:53.710
The attack occurs when a merchant works with
Seven on the installation of a weapons system

00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:55.150
aboard Voyager.

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:59.490
During their time together, he expresses interest
in the nanoprobe technology that powers her

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:03.300
Borg implants and is told that they are not
for sale.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:07.440
But after Seven attacks the visitor, she finds
herself in sickbay where she has an acute

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:08.480
panic attack.

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:13.000
The Doctor helps her recover repressed memories
of Kovin forcibly extracting the nanoprobes

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:14.290
against her will.

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:18.480
While there are many moments in "Retrospect"
that speak to the real traumas faced by victims

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:22.160
of sex crimes, Star Trek: Voyager goes too
far with the premise.

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:26.400
They turn what could have been a powerful
episode about sexual assault and how allegations

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:30.910
are too often ignored into a story about the
wrongfully accused and how recovered memories

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:32.840
can spark false allegations.

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:37.670
"Retrospect" chose not to focus on an important
issue and found itself sending all the wrong

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:38.860
messages.

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:43.150
If you or someone you know has been the victim
of sexual assault, you can report it by calling

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:48.260
the National Sexual Assault Hotline 1-800-656-4673.

